Negative control for the BDD anchor gate, third direction.

The two committed directions catch a declared capability nobody proved, and a
spec naming an id its feature never declared. Neither sees the third shape: a
spec that anchors a FEATURE and claims none of its ids. That anchor reads as
traceability in every report and answers no capability at all, which is how
`switch-session-scope.spec.ts` sat pointed at `session-switcher.feature` while
proving `A7` — a scenario that feature does not carry.

The mutant restores exactly that line. Nothing else changes: every scenario
keeps the spec that answers it, so both committed directions stay GREEN and only
the new one can see this.

This gate's subject IS the anchor graph — the features and the specs that cite
them — so its mutant lives where the defect lived. That is the same reason the
sibling control mutates a `.feature` rather than production code: for this gate
the declaration corpus is the source under test.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Expected RED (module lane):
  feature-traceability.spec.ts  "leaves no spec anchoring a feature while
                                  claiming none of its capabilities"
                                -> ["…/switch-session-scope.spec.ts @anchor
                                     session-switcher.feature"]

